## 1.2.2. The `for` Loop

The `for` loop is a common way to iterate over a range of values.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int i = 0;
    for (i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
        printf("i: %d\n", i);
    }
    return 0;
}
```

## 1.2.3. The `while` Loop

The `while` loop is a common way to iterate over a range of values.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int i = 0;
    while (i < 10) {
        printf("i: %d\n", i);
        i++;
    }
    return 0;
}
```

## 1.2.4. The `do` Loop

The `do` loop is a common way to iterate over a range of values.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int i = 0;
    do {
        printf("i: %d\n", i);
        i++;
    } while (i < 10);
    return 0;
}
```

## 1.2.5. The `break` and `continue` Statements

The `break` statement is used to exit a loop.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int i = 0;
    while (i < 10) {
        printf("i: %d\n", i);
        i++;
        if (i == 5) {
            break;
        }
    }
    return 0;
}
```

The `continue` statement is used to skip the rest of the current iteration of a loop.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int i = 0;
    while (i < 10) {
        printf("i: %d\n", i);
        i++;
        if (i == 5) {
            continue;
        }
    }
    return 0;
}
```

## 1.2.6. The `goto` Statement

The `goto` statement is used to jump to a specific label.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int i = 0;
    while (i < 10) {
        printf("i: %d\n", i);
        i++;
        if (i == 5) {
            goto label;
        }
    }
    label:
    printf("label\n");
    return 0;
}
```

## 1.2.7. The `switch` Statement

The `switch` statement is used to select a case from a list of cases.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int i = 0;
    switch (i) {
        case 0:
            printf("case 0\n");
            break;
        case 1:
            printf("case 1\n");
            break;
        case 2:
            printf("case 2\n");
            break;
        default:
            printf("default\n");
            break;
    }
    return 0;
}
```

