## 1.2.1. The `for` Loop

The `for` loop is a common way to repeat a block of code a certain number of times.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    // print the numbers 1 through 10
    for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
        printf("%d\n", i);
    }
}
```

The `for` loop has three parts:

- The initialization statement, which is executed only once.
- The condition statement, which is evaluated before each iteration.
- The update statement, which is executed after each iteration.

The initialization statement is optional, but the condition statement is required.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    // print the numbers 1 through 10
    for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
        printf("%d\n", i);
    }
}
```

The update statement is optional.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    // print the numbers 1 through 10
    for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
        printf("%d\n", i);
    }
}
```

The `for` loop is often used to iterate over a list of items.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    char *vehicles[] = {"boat", "car", "truck", "train"};

    // print each vehicle
    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
        printf("%s\n", vehicles[i]);
    }
}
```

## 1.2.2. The `while` Loop

The `while` loop is similar to the `for` loop, but it only executes the block of code once.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    // print the numbers 1 through 10
    int i = 1;
    while (i <= 10) {
        printf("%d\n", i);
        i++;
    }
}
```

The `while` loop has two parts:

- The condition statement, which is evaluated before each iteration.
- The block of code, which is executed after each iteration.

The condition statement is required.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    // print the numbers 1 through 10
    int i = 1;
    while (i <= 10) {
        printf("%d\n", i);
        i++;
    }
}
```

The block of code is required.

